Upgrades to Newer PHP and Libraries
***********************************

.[perex]
The `upgrading` section decides on upgrading code to a newer PHP and to new library versions: what a newer PHP can write better, what it deprecated and what to replace it with, and what the libraries your project uses have renamed or removed.

The decisions of the section are grouped by what they are about: `syntax` covers new syntax (`?->`, `match`, the pipe operator, promoted properties, `readonly`), `functions` new functions (`str_contains()`, `array_any()`, `clamp()`), `classes` attributes and interfaces that PHP added (`#[\Override]`, `Stringable`), `phpdoc` annotations that an attribute or a keyword replaced, `php` what PHP deprecated, `api` replacement maps for the API of libraries and of your own code, and `declarations` uses of what your own code marked `@deprecated`. The value `required` turns on the newer form ("write the newer form wherever you find the older one"), and the value `forbidden` turns on replacing a deprecated form ("the deprecated form must not be here").

```neon
upgrading:
	functions:
		substringFunctions: required
	syntax:
		nullsafe: required
		pipe: required
```

```php .[before]
function describe(?User $user, string $url): string
{
	if (strpos($url, 'https://') === 0) {  // The `strpos()` comparison must be written with `str_starts_with()`.
		$scheme = 'secure';
	}
	$name = $user === null ? null : $user->getName();  // The ternary testing for null must be written with `?->`.
	return trim(strtolower(strip_tags($name)));  // The nested calls must be written with the pipe operator.
}
```

```php .[after]
function describe(?User $user, string $url): string
{
	if (str_starts_with($url, 'https://')) {
		$scheme = 'secure';
	}
	$name = $user?->getName();
	return $name |> strip_tags(...) |> strtolower(...) |> trim(...);
}
```

Each decision that writes newer PHP code knows which PHP version introduced it, and it applies only when the [target PHP version of the project |/configuration#PHP and Package Versions] allows it. The pipe operator from the example gets into the code only when the `composer.json` of the project allows nothing older than PHP 8.5; until then, `dresscode config` prints `no effect: php` next to the decision. Deprecations, on the other hand, are always reported, because code written for an older PHP will one day run on a version that has deprecated it.

You do not have to write the decisions of the section one by one. The `modernizations` set turns on everything that writes a newer form, and the `deprecations` set everything that fixes deprecated code. You add both on top of the standard in the [`use` |/configuration#Standards and Sets] key, and as DressCode gains new decisions, the sets bring them in without any change to your configuration. [Upgrading to a Newer PHP |/upgrading-php] shows what upgrading to a newer PHP looks like in practice, and [Code Upgrade |/upgrade] shows how to upgrade libraries.


PHP Migration Guides
====================

DressCode carries what PHP deprecated, and how the same thing is written today, as the data of the package `php`. This data has the same format as the upgrade data of libraries in the upgrade packages: a deprecated function or constant is reported, `utf8_encode()` is rewritten to `mb_convert_encoding()`, and a call that does nothing on the target version, such as `curl_close()`, disappears. A single requirement, `upgrading.php.deprecatedApi`, turns on the whole guide, and everything the data finds is reported under it, so this is also the name you use in `--only`, `severity`, `fixRisky` or in a `dresscode:ignore` comment. To exclude a single item of the data, set it to `keep` in the map that the item belongs to:

```neon
upgrading:
	php:
		deprecatedApi: forbidden
	api:
		replacedCalls:
			'curl_close($handle)': keep
```

No map can name an argument value that PHP deprecated, such as `E_USER_ERROR` passed to `trigger_error()` or an integer passed to a `ctype_*()` function. A separate decision, `upgrading.php.deprecatedArgument`, reports such a value. The `nette` standard and the `deprecations` set turn on both decisions.


API Replacement Maps
====================

The keys under `upgrading.api` are replacement maps: a class for a class, a member for a member, a call for a template, a forbidden function with advice on what to write instead. A project writes them when it renames its own code, and upgrade packages carry them for whole ecosystems. The decision `upgrading.packages: yes`, which both sets, `deprecations` and `modernizations`, turn on, adds the package data as a layer below the maps of the project. The value `keep` takes an item the project does not want out of the maps (`Acme\Mail\SmtpTransport: keep`), and an exclamation mark after a map key (`replacedClasses!:`) replaces the maps of lower layers and the package data with what the project writes. The page [Replacement Maps |/upgrading-data] describes the grammar of the maps.


upgrading.php.floatArrayKey
===========================

`$a[(int) $ratio]` and `[1 => 'x']`, where a float key loses its fraction, deprecated by PHP 8.1.

- `forbidden`: never there
- `keep`: nothing is enforced, the code stays as it is

Default `keep` · standards: perCs `keep`, psr12 `keep`, nette `keep`, symfony `keep`, laravel `keep` .[decision-facts]


